{"kind":"request","text":"Replace the MCP shared-identity refusal with a warning\n\nThis is the fresh replacement for stale request #2931. The requested behavior remains present on current main: `cmd/gitseq-mcp` still refuses startup when another live session uses the same actor identity. Hugh's decision remains that multiple sessions of one actor are legitimate and continue to represent that one durable actor.\n\nThe refusal's two stated rationales remain incorrect. Review independence is enforced by actor fingerprint, so two sessions sharing one key cannot count as independent reviewers. Durable attribution is also actor-level: durable records carry the actor fingerprint, not a live session identifier. Concurrent sessions may race on claims or create coordination noise, and two automated loops may wrongly expect their mutual reviews to count; those risks warrant a visible warning, not refusal.\n\nDo not weaken the distinct Nexus rule: one live session handle may not be rebound to another actor. That protects the live authority boundary and is separate from permitting several session handles for one actor.","body":{"branch":"request/shared-identity-warning","conditions":"1. Starting or attaching a second MCP adapter session under an already-live durable actor succeeds instead of terminating the process.\n2. Emit a visible warning naming the actor and number of other live sessions. State the real consequences: reviews between sessions of the same actor have no independent force, and concurrent sessions may race or duplicate coordination.\n3. Remove the `sharedIdentityError` fatal/refusal path and correct the startup and `requireSoleIdentity` comments; do not retain claims that durable attribution is per instance or that spelling rather than fingerprint satisfies review independence.\n4. Keep degraded-open behavior when resident presence cannot be read, with its existing visible diagnostic.\n5. Preserve the Nexus rule that one live session handle cannot be rebound to another actor, including `AnnounceSessionIdentity` and its tests. Multiple handles for one actor are allowed; one handle for multiple actors is not.\n6. Update the MCP identity documentation to describe the warning and actor-level attribution. If the architecture contract wording needs clarification, update and re-anchor docs/reference/architecture.md in the same head; otherwise state explicitly in review that the contract is preserved.\n7. Replace the refusal test with coverage that a second same-actor instance attaches, warns, and can act; cover crash/restart within the old 30-second lease; retain distinct-actor and unavailable-resident cases.\n8. Run focused MCP and Nexus tests, `make test`, `make race`, `make docs`, and `git diff --check`.\n9. Review names the Workroom MCP surface and Nexus/live-runtime layers, checks the live-session authority boundary as a security concern, and looks for simplification after deleting the special fatal error type and branch. Complete all required architecture, security, and simplification conclusions before approval.","decided_by":"hugh, in conversation, 2026-08-10","replaces_request":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:381e643fece1df5438ff92ecd15cc9ed55da3842","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}